    "title": "Veteran Game Show Host Wink Martindale Passes Away at 91",
    "text": "• Winston 'Wink' Martindale has died at the age of 91.\n• He hosted over a dozen game shows during his career, including 'Tic-Tac-Dough,' 'Gambit,' and 'High Rollers.'\n• Martindale was battling lymphoma for a year before his death.\n• He passed away at Eisenhower Health in Rancho Mirage, California.\n• Born on December 4, 1933, in Jackson, Tennessee.\n• Martindale's interest in entertainment began at a young age, influenced by his Sunday school teacher.\n• He started his broadcasting career at the age of 17.\n• He hosted children's programs in Memphis, including 'Mars Patrol' and 'Teenage Dance Party.'\n• Martindale interviewed Elvis Presley early in his career and maintained a friendship with him.\n• He moved to Los Angeles in 1959 and began hosting morning radio shows.\n• His first network game show was NBC's 'What's This Song?'\n• He hosted various game shows, including 'Dream Girl '67,' 'How's Your Mother-in-Law?,' 'Trivial Pursuit,' and 'Debt.'\n• Martindale founded Wink Martindale Enterprises and published a memoir titled 'Winking at Life' in 2000.\n• He enjoyed meeting people through game shows and had a broadcasting career spanning over 70 years.\n• Martindale is survived by his wife Sandra, three daughters (Lisa, Lyn, and Laura), and his sister Geraldine.",
